WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.616
What are students actually supposed to learn?

2
00:00:02.616 --> 00:00:06.337
It's a crowded list, and every subject has passionate defenders.

3
00:00:06.337 --> 00:00:12.763
It starts with standards: statements of what students should know and be able to do in each grade.

4
00:00:12.763 --> 00:00:21.811
In 2010, California adopted the Common Core State Standards in English and math, which emphasize reasoning and evidence over memorization.

5
00:00:21.811 --> 00:00:31.449
The state has its own standards in science, based on the Next Generation Science Standards, and frameworks for history, the arts, health, and more.

6
00:00:31.449 --> 00:00:33.285
Standards describe the goal.

7
00:00:33.285 --> 00:00:41.219
Curriculum is the specific materials and lessons, and that's mostly chosen by each district from lists the state reviews.

8
00:00:41.219 --> 00:00:44.825
A common worry is whether school is challenging enough.

9
00:00:44.825 --> 00:00:49.415
Students can earn good grades while doing work well below grade level.

10
00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:56.955
Studies of real classroom assignments often find that many are too easy for the standards they're supposed to meet.

11
00:00:56.955 --> 00:01:20.100
There's a concrete way to see this in high school: California's public universities, the UC and CSU systems, require a specific sequence of fifteen courses known as the "a-g" requirements, covering subjects from history to lab science to a world language, and only roughly half of California's high school graduates actually complete that full sequence.

12
00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:22.264
Rigor doesn't mean more homework.

13
00:01:22.264 --> 00:01:25.018
It means work that asks students to think.

14
00:01:25.018 --> 00:01:28.071
Reading is the foundation of everything else.

15
00:01:28.071 --> 00:01:34.856
A child who can't read well by around third grade has a much harder time in every subject afterward.

16
00:01:34.856 --> 00:01:45.915
There's been a national shift toward what's called the science of reading: explicit, systematic teaching of phonics, alongside vocabulary and background knowledge.

17
00:01:45.915 --> 00:01:56.838
California has moved in this direction with new laws requiring early screening for reading difficulties, including dyslexia, and investments in literacy coaches.

18
00:01:56.838 --> 00:02:03.691
Math is the subject where California students struggle most, and where pandemic losses were steepest.

19
00:02:03.691 --> 00:02:15.021
The state adopted a revised math framework in 2023 after a heated debate about how to balance access for all students with acceleration for those ready to move faster.

20
00:02:15.021 --> 00:02:23.570
Science, technology, and engineering round out STEM, and access to advanced courses still varies widely from school to school.

21
00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.525
Tests are how the system checks whether students are learning.

22
00:02:27.525 --> 00:02:35.242
California's main state tests are called CAASPP, taken each spring in grades three through eight and once in high school.

23
00:02:35.242 --> 00:02:41.302
English learners also take a separate test, the ELPAC, that measures their progress in English.

24
00:02:41.302 --> 00:02:44.044
Tests have real limits and can be overused.

25
00:02:44.044 --> 00:02:51.188
But without some common measure, it's hard to know which students need help or which schools are falling behind.

26
00:02:51.188 --> 00:02:59.224
Technology has transformed how schools deliver lessons, especially since the pandemic, when nearly every student got a device.

27
00:02:59.224 --> 00:03:10.131
The evidence is clear that technology helps when it's used for specific purposes, like practice with instant feedback, and less clear when screens simply replace teachers.

28
00:03:10.131 --> 00:03:29.585
Having a device isn't the whole story, though: many lower-income students still face what's called the homework gap, relying on public WiFi or limited, unreliable internet at home to get schoolwork done, which is why school-provided devices and connectivity matter just as much as what's in the classroom.

29
00:03:29.585 --> 00:03:37.749
Artificial intelligence is the newest wave, and schools are still working out rules for how students and teachers should use it.

30
00:03:37.749 --> 00:03:42.239
Then there's everything that makes school feel worth attending.

31
00:03:42.239 --> 00:03:47.869
Engagement rises when students see a connection between school and their lives.

32
00:03:47.869 --> 00:03:55.494
The arts, music, drama, visual art, and dance, build creativity and give many students a reason to show up.

33
00:03:55.494 --> 00:04:03.334
In 2022, California voters passed Proposition 28, which dedicates ongoing funding to arts and music education.

34
00:04:03.334 --> 00:04:27.778
Physical education and sports build health, teamwork, and focus, and the state has recently put real weight behind unstructured play too: Senate Bill 291, sometimes called "Recess for All," took effect for the 2024-25 school year and requires at least thirty minutes of daily recess while barring schools from taking recess away as punishment.

35
00:04:27.778 --> 00:04:33.551
Field trips expose kids to places and ideas they might never otherwise encounter.

36
00:04:33.551 --> 00:04:43.956
Career technical education, sometimes called CTE, connects learning to jobs in fields like health care, construction, technology, and agriculture.

37
00:04:43.956 --> 00:04:55.572
Many high schools now offer career pathways, internships, and dual enrollment, where students take community college classes for credit while still in high school.

38
00:04:55.572 --> 00:04:58.608
Some subjects are about who students become.

39
00:04:58.608 --> 00:05:06.404
Social-emotional learning, or SEL, teaches skills like managing emotions, setting goals, and working with others.

40
00:05:06.404 --> 00:05:10.889
Service learning connects academic work to helping the community.

41
00:05:10.889 --> 00:05:15.305
Character education aims at habits like honesty and persistence.

42
00:05:15.305 --> 00:05:22.204
These areas are sometimes controversial, with debates about whether schools or families should lead.

43
00:05:22.204 --> 00:05:42.489
Civics, history, and geography prepare students to understand and participate in democracy, and the stakes are higher than they might seem: surveys have found that only about half of American adults can name all three branches of government, and roughly a quarter can't name any of them at all.

44
00:05:42.489 --> 00:05:50.009
California offers a State Seal of Civic Engagement to recognize students who show civic knowledge and action.

45
00:05:50.009 --> 00:05:54.425
World languages matter in a state as multilingual as California.

46
00:05:54.425 --> 00:06:15.262
After voters passed Proposition 58 in 2016, schools gained more freedom to offer bilingual and dual-language programs, where students learn in two languages, and that measure effectively reversed an earlier one, Proposition 227 from 1998, which had required English-only instruction in most classrooms.

47
00:06:15.262 --> 00:06:21.402
Students who master two languages can earn the State Seal of Biliteracy on their diploma.

48
00:06:21.402 --> 00:06:24.093
And a newer addition: personal finance.

49
00:06:24.093 --> 00:06:34.788
California passed a law requiring high school students to complete a semester of personal finance before graduating, being phased in over the coming years.

50
00:06:34.788 --> 00:06:38.513
Budgets, credit, and loans, finally in the curriculum.

51
00:06:38.513 --> 00:06:42.791
For more, see Chapter 6 of the free Ed100 course at ed100.org.
